  "query_preview": "..\nTonight: Scattered showers and thunderstorms along the East\nSlopes of the Cascades and northern mountains will continue to\ndevelop into the evening hours due to increased lift and\ninstability with a passing upper level trough. Precipitable\nwater values around 150% of normal and slow storm motion will\nallow for rain to occur with these storms, which could be\nlocally heavy along the East Slopes of the Cascades and Okanogan\nHighlands where storm motion is slowest. If a storm goes over a\nvulnerable burn scar debris flows would be possible and \nsomething that will continue to be monitored. Elevated\ninstability over NE WA into the ID Panhandle will allow for some\nstorms to persist into the overnight hours and thus the Red Flag\nWarning for much of NE WA/N Idaho was extended through 8 AM\nThursday. This passing weather system will also allow a backdoor\ncold front to move in from the north and northeast with gusty\nwinds developing this evening. Several of the high resolution\nmodels show widespread gusts of 25-40 MPH developing and thus\nevening winds were increased. As of 2 PM some of these gusts\nwere already materializing with gusts to around 35 MPH in the\nOkanogan Valley and 25 MPH around Bonners Ferry.\n\nThursday: As the short wave associated with the upper trough\nexits large scale lift moves out. However residual moisture and\ndaytime heating is expected to allow isolated showers and\nthunderstorms to develop over the high terrain, especially along\nthe East Slopes of the Cascades.  \n\nFriday through Wednesday: Ensemble means show the upper trough\nretrograding to off the coast putting the Inland NW in a broad\nsouth to southwest flow for several days. We will need to\nclosely watch for additional weather disturbances coming through\nthe region. Yet at this time most of these look on the weaker\nside supporting the chance of daily afternoon and evening\nthunderstorm chances mainly over the mountains.  \n\nRegarding smoke: The strong shift in north to northeast this\nevening, with winds remaining out of the north-northeast into\nThursday and Friday may help clear some smoke out of the \nregion, especially over NE Washington and N Idaho. Yet with the \npotential for new fire starts from lightning this is not a high \nconfidence situation. Several Air Quality agencies have issued \nAir Quality Alerts and Advisories which remain in effect for \nseveral counties. JW",
